{"title":"2014 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 22","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2014 AMC 12B Problems\">2014 AMC 12B #22</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2014 AMC 10B Problems\">2014 AMC 10B #25</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Explaining_the_Martingale_Shortcut_Mentioned_Previously)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Explaining the Martingale Shortcut Mentioned Previously)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Step_1:_Find_the_Step_Rule\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 1: Find the Step Rule</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Step_2:_Build_the_Chain_of_Gaps\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 2: Build the Chain of Gaps</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Step_3:_Find_the_Total_Score_and_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Step 3: Find the Total Score and Solve</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Remark_(Markov_Chain)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark (Markov Chain)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In a small pond there are eleven lily pads in a row labeled 0 through 10.  A frog is sitting on pad 1.  When the frog is on pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/8/c78f961646cafb20c51dc39f3c154fbf17265b4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;N&lt;10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"91\" height=\"13\" />, it will jump to pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/5/a256c70ad4c46ec1127c5be68f8bb3075e9ced31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" /> with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c34b06249aac069179cec1997539a6c74799bba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{N}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"38\" /> and to pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/f/a7fc8f668ffc0730528bca6447b71e28cebd0cf0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" /> with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/9/0a92a16d0a76cb702e9c77ece07a2fd85d63c747.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\frac{N}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"52\" height=\"38\" />.  Each jump is independent of the previous jumps.  If the frog reaches pad 0 it will be eaten by a patiently waiting snake.  If the frog reaches pad 10 it will exit the pond, never to return.  What is the probability that the frog will escape without being eaten by the snake?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64ac910622760606eba973537ab91fe236af0ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }\\frac{32}{79}\\qquad \\textbf{(B) }\\frac{161}{384}\\qquad \\textbf{(C) }\\frac{63}{146}\\qquad \\textbf{(D) }\\frac{7}{16}\\qquad \\textbf{(E) }\\frac{1}{2}\\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"463\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A long, but straightforward bash:\n</p><p>Define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a3c6a3a3cae21e33f051e2e292b0bc344d576f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(N)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> to be the probability that the frog survives starting from pad N.\n</p><p>Then note that by symmetry, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/3/b73903f4745f1074c59735ca1b097f504a51a93b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(5) = 1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" />, since the probabilities of the frog moving subsequently in either direction from pad 5 are equal.\n</p><p>We therefore seek to rewrite <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/7/797a20647c81f3259c5069556cab51c94bf4b752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d2840f4c4a51ffb604727078a50a895034a531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />, using the fact that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/0/d102691a3eb25222b388c725c17c6f0759171bce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(N) = \\frac {N} {10}P(N - 1) + \\frac {10 - N} {10}P(N + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"325\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>as said in the problem.\n</p><p>Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/ccef8330954c5607d5e59e4da8ca04351c4548b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1) = \\frac {1} {10}P(0) + \\frac {9} {10}P(2) = \\frac {9} {10}P(2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"285\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58cb799beff88a0f046809b4ffc79e16febbbcdf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Rightarrow P(2) = \\frac {10} {9}P(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"143\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Returning to our original equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/a/05af66a39fca6a403557ec1dc985744f761180d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1) = \\frac {9} {10}P(2) = \\frac {9} {10}\\left(\\frac{2} {10}P(1) + \\frac{8} {10}P(3)\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"334\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/8/0f8b2b75dd538d85b2e9044771d48d0ff32aaf8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {9} {50}P(1) + \\frac {18} {25}P(3) \\Rightarrow P(1) - \\frac {9} {50}P(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"306\" height=\"37\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/0/ed0094d53e75030e4e6e1101db7d82dd9b09b83b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {18} {25}P(3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"77\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/7/e7728de2981939d6cc4aa9512399b623a967451e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Rightarrow P(3) = \\frac {41} {36}P(1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"143\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Returning to our original equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/d/53d9c2adebd7c1185d76f589533dcccb76e7906b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1) = \\frac {9} {50}P(1) + \\frac {18} {25}\\left(\\frac {3} {10}P(2) + \\frac {7} {10}P(4)\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"332\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/6/f46275bcaaf6ec12ec0a32dcfaa16ef58099d63b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {9} {50}P(1) + \\frac {27} {125}P(2) + \\frac {63} {125}P(4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"259\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcbe0f38bf5498db55874ff9733059ba84ea7dce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {9} {50}P(1) + \\frac {27} {125}\\left(\\frac {10} {9}P(1)\\right) + \\frac {63} {125}\\left(\\frac {4} {10}P(3) + \\frac {6} {10}P(5)\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"441\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Cleaning up the coefficients, we have: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/3/5539ccc2183448bfb86eb8f57ac261e43d594ca7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {21} {50}P(1) + \\frac {126} {625}P(3) + \\frac {189} {625}P(5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"259\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/6/776107c6add63d6b047b2ccd74522b045c2ea03c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\frac {21} {50}P(1) + \\frac {126} {625}\\left(\\frac {41} {36}P(1)\\right) + \\frac {189} {625}\\left(\\frac {1} {2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"313\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/d/0dda986f99ad6efebab3e9d051d8ddcecd3d165d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1) = \\frac {525} {1250}P(1) + \\frac {287} {1250}P(1) + \\frac {189} {1250}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"300\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/3/103138e22f633b4661ddd39c6e030681fd976728.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Rightarrow P(1) - \\frac {812} {1250}P(1) = \\frac {189} {1250} \\Rightarrow P(1) = \\frac {189} {438}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"348\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/40033c2a1cf5ac46f78ded6462bcd1212e9ab89e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= \\boxed{\\frac {63} {146}\\, (C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"93\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>Or set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/d/26dc1fee3126f1cfa247981081b69d4fccc41a07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(1)=a,P(2)=b,P(3)=c,P(4)=d,P(5)=e=1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"434\" height=\"18\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/c/b2c5d894909662c743bd8cc51b9f3fb826e03719.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a=0.1\\emptyset+0.9b,b=0.2a+0.8c,c=0.3b+0.7d,d=0.4c+0.6e\\]\" width=\"478\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/b/8ab0be04234eaf7cf5e39b42867e86027faba272.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10a=\\emptyset+9b,10b=2a+8c,10c=3b+7d,10d=4c+6e\\]\" width=\"447\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/0/8c05a46d83b68bceac26aca6051924152c138c55.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies b=\\frac{10a-\\emptyset}{9},c=\\frac{5b-a}{4},d=\\frac{10c-3b}{7},e=\\frac{5d-2c}{3}=1/2\\]\" width=\"490\" height=\"40\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e6766e648c5f17ff1a97cccfa073c57ba39d42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=\\frac{10a}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"62\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/d/82db557d2bf2d95837d8bbc1f36e2f89c8ee8164.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=\\frac{5\\left(\\frac{10a}{9}\\right)-a}{4}=\\frac{\\frac{50a}{9}-a}{9}=\\frac{41a}{36}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"259\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab12aeba196236ecf3bcfc131b3c119549bff40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d=\\frac{10\\left(\\frac{41a}{36}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{30a}{9}\\right)}{7}=\\frac{\\frac{205a}{18}-\\frac{10a}{3}}{7}=\\frac{145a}{126}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"344\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/6/d161be7f3b840a8b0b797d40b9193c55bc434332.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e=\\frac{5\\left(\\frac{145a}{126}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{41a}{36}\\right)}{3}=\\frac{\\frac{725a}{126}-\\frac{41a}{18}}{3}=\\frac{73a}{63}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"331\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/c/59cfed396a22c040f22cee3d77edfb04e58d9639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e=\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b3918aa2e7e86ea58c170f49c0e7a5ca3017b00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{73a}{63}=\\frac{1}{2}\\implies a=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }\\frac{63}{146}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"230\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that the probabilities are symmetrical around the fifth lily pad. If the frog is on the fifth lily pad, there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance that it escapes and a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance that it gets eaten. Now, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/e/76ebaba4ae3d5c911d6e31ad855d502ad88d48a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> represent the probability that the frog escapes if it is currently on pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. We get the following system of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equations:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c2e4c28a45884b84086cb6eb0f167d4d3e69db5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_1=\\frac{9}{10}\\cdot P_2\\]\" width=\"95\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/b/2bbb67cd6b1c50a72365ec5e6dde4a98db920cbf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_2=\\frac{2}{10}\\cdot P_1 + \\frac{8}{10}\\cdot P_3\\]\" width=\"172\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e65e1fbaa78bf606934653934fefca3a56818f16.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_3=\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot P_2 + \\frac{7}{10}\\cdot P_4\\]\" width=\"173\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12ce201d1dc74685eaade813834f7619f9e33ac1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_4=\\frac{4}{10}\\cdot P_3 + \\frac{6}{10}\\cdot P_5\\]\" width=\"172\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/c/98c1cdb7052a4bf6932e805224a4e3f9b036822b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P_5=\\frac{5}{10}\\]\" width=\"63\" height=\"38\" />\nWe want to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />, since the frog starts at pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Solving the above system yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efddbc2a71d6ee78ce7b8c7f301b691431500de3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1=\\frac{63}{146}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"73\" height=\"38\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/b/6dbbac62a58811f44fda1df541fe2d2b7f43bc64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"40\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p>System of equations (alligator112): Start by removing all fractions. From here on out, we will use substitution while avoiding fractions.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/5/7d508dc769f73ec9649b101abb0b08c7ff44f8e4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10P_1 = 9P_2\\]\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/0/a60f31838795413c8c90126b2b592a04ac4b4de6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10P_2 = 2P_1 + 8P_3\\]\" width=\"137\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d342fd37618bbc55c7901e9c9fad713c3562175e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10P_3 = 3P_2 + 7P_4\\]\" width=\"137\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/d/7ed7280295e556699a5df6f3d52154398edc8f3d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[10P_4 = 4P_3 + 6P_5 = 4P_3 + 3\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/7/1473688930c484578d163b08172a76ee5b8ea1d5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[100 P_3 = 30P_2 + 70P_4 = 30P_2 + 7(4P_3 + 3) \\rightarrow 72P_3 = 30P_2 + 21\\]\" width=\"499\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/2/33276bac22db8742d187bfa8fae4e363e741a9d9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[90P_2 = 18P_1 + 72P_3 = 18P_1 + 30P_2 + 21 \\rightarrow 60P_2 = 18P_1 + 21\\]\" width=\"485\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/5/dd57f60b5bfb9477a96c7e06758443a30ce4f9d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[200P_1 = 180P_2 = 3(18P_1 + 21) = 54P_1 + 63 \\rightarrow P_1 = \\frac{63}{146}\\]\" width=\"444\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assign each lily pad a value, with pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> having value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/d/25dfa5e1205b2ae736cbc053851e5518ca1d554e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" /> having value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b53aa9144cae6739984cacd5bc03a461883a01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{k=0}^{i-1}\\frac{1}{\\binom{9}{k}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"58\" height=\"56\" />. If we treat the process as a random walk <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/7/e57aa074447ecae8a2beedb51b2ff97e8c3c513d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_0, X_1, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> over these values, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/6/4967285270d715e70ef7576ce3d2b24791e748e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[X_{n+1}] = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"100\" height=\"20\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/c/e2c7d62172af0e738dd12f47f3f8ad8ef70cd65f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />. This is purely by construction, as the expected gain at pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/9/d1933942c87799c99961e274e092da305e0b158e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{9}{k}\\cdot \\frac{10-k}{10} - \\binom{9}{k-1}\\cdot \\frac{k}{10} = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"268\" height=\"55\" />. Therefore the process is a martingale. Since the stopping time has finite expectation and the increments are bounded, the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optional_stopping_theorem\">Optional Stopping Thoerem</a> applies and the expected final outcome is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This gives the probability of reaching safety as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be51f9cae7ac181dfbc45d499dce3647603bfe79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\sum_{k=0}^{9}\\frac{1}{\\binom{9}{k}}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{63}{146}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"160\" height=\"59\" />, which is choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/b/6dbbac62a58811f44fda1df541fe2d2b7f43bc64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{(C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"40\" height=\"29\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Explaining_the_Martingale_Shortcut_Mentioned_Previously.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Explaining_the_Martingale_Shortcut_Mentioned_Previously)\">Solution 4 (Explaining the Martingale Shortcut Mentioned Previously)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Explaining the Martingale Shortcut Mentioned Previously)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Instead of grinding through a massive system of 9 equations, we can solve this problem by assigning a \"score value\" to each lily pad. \n</p><p>Normally, if a game is perfectly fair (like a 50/50 coin flip), your average score after a turn is the same as your score before the turn. But this frog pond is rigged because the probabilities change at every pad. To fix this, we can mathematically design a custom scoring system that forces the game to be perfectly fair. \n</p><p>Once the scoring system is perfectly balanced, the law of expected value tells us that the probability of winning is just a simple ratio:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/2/d528ae4b2207ba9dd15e21dc2087545c8e192b46.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Escape Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Score of your Starting Pad}}{\\text{Score of the Winning Pad (Pad 10)}}\\]\" width=\"420\" height=\"40\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>(Note: We set the score of Pad 0 to be 0 points, so getting eaten by the snake gives 0 credit).*</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_1:_Find_the_Step_Rule\">Step 1: Find the Step Rule</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Step 1: Find the Step Rule\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>To keep our scores perfectly balanced at any pad <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, the reward for a rare forward jump must perfectly offset the risk of an easy backward jump. This gives us a simple rule for how the point-gaps between pads must scale:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e2186b1698d87fda4568ec46f5679234dde9ec5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Step Multiplier} = \\frac{\\text{Probability of jumping Left}}{\\text{Probability of jumping Right}} = \\frac{\\frac{N}{10}}{\\frac{10-N}{10}} = \\frac{N}{10-N}\\]\" width=\"500\" height=\"47\" />\n</p><p>This fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/d/74da5d328835e0dfb6009089b34cb4eaf2dea896.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{N}{10-N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"63\" height=\"41\" /> tells us exactly how much larger the next point-step must be to keep our scoring scale balanced.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_2:_Build_the_Chain_of_Gaps\">Step 2: Build the Chain of Gaps</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Step 2: Build the Chain of Gaps\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We start by declaring that moving from Pad 0 to Pad 1 awards exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> point. To find the point value of each subsequent step, we take our current step value and multiply it by our Step Multiplier rule:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>Step 1 (Pad 0 to 1):</b> Worth <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> point. Pad 1 Score = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li><b>Step 2 (Pad 1 to 2):</b> Multiply by the rule at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/d/41db93e7b2e23f17a0a24f08017230f4c0c1cfc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=1 \\implies 1 \\times \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"183\" height=\"38\" /> points.</li>\n<li><b>Step 3 (Pad 2 to 3):</b> Multiply by the rule at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb3d5de1600516b0b7bd272dac77fe84f8056e70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=2 \\implies \\frac{1}{9} \\times \\frac{2}{8} = \\frac{1}{36}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"196\" height=\"38\" /> points.</li>\n<li><b>Step 4 (Pad 3 to 4):</b> Multiply by the rule at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/d/1cd49d0d3a0fe037881e70752ea5b8ebb0622640.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=3 \\implies \\frac{1}{36} \\times \\frac{3}{7} = \\frac{1}{84}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"205\" height=\"38\" /> points.</li>\n<li><b>Step 5 (Pad 4 to 5):</b> Multiply by the rule at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/0/260e6839c50ed0c22eb3ea392b5aec57c16198fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=4 \\implies \\frac{1}{84} \\times \\frac{4}{6} = \\frac{1}{126}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"214\" height=\"38\" /> points.</li></ul>\n<p>Because the pond's layout is perfectly symmetric, the point values for the remaining five steps mirror these numbers exactly in reverse order. This gives us all 10 step values:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/3/5f36ae90b2f35984add002c66360bf705c3209af.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left[ 1, \\, \\frac{1}{9}, \\, \\frac{1}{36}, \\, \\frac{1}{84}, \\, \\frac{1}{126}, \\, \\frac{1}{126}, \\, \\frac{1}{84}, \\, \\frac{1}{36}, \\, \\frac{1}{9}, \\, 1 \\right]\\]\" width=\"305\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Step_3:_Find_the_Total_Score_and_Solve\">Step 3: Find the Total Score and Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Step 3: Find the Total Score and Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Now we can easily calculate our two target scores:\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Score of our Starting Pad (Pad 1):</b> This is just the value of the very first step: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li><b>Score of the Winning Pad (Pad 10):</b> This is the total sum of all 10 steps combined. Grouping the identical matching pairs over a common denominator of 126 makes this simple to calculate:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb25c21cc89e9aa86e66ec22778a7b4d5e150e36.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Total Score of Pad 10} = 2 \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{1}{36} + \\frac{1}{84} + \\frac{1}{126}\\right) = 2 \\left(\\frac{252 + 28 + 7 + 3 + 2}{126}\\right) = \\frac{146}{63}\\]\" width=\"688\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Finally, we divide our starting score by the winning pad's score to get the final probability:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/c/29c11046ac8eb71d1d155e36e1a012019d186db8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = \\frac{\\text{Starting Score}}{\\text{Winning Score}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{146}{63}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{63}{146}}\\]\" width=\"303\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User%EF%BC%9AIcosahedron0288#Created_Pages\" title=\"User：Icosahedron0288\">Icosahedron0288</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remark_.28Markov_Chain.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark_(Markov_Chain)\">Remark (Markov Chain)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Remark (Markov Chain)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<dl><dd><i>Main article: <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Markov_Chains\" title=\"Markov Chains\">Markov Chains</a></i></dd></dl>\n<p>We can represent this problem with the following State Transition Diagram of <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absorbing_Markov_chain\">Absorbing Markov Chain</a>. State <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> are the absorbing states. This problem asks for the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absorbing_Markov_chain#Absorbing_probabilities\">Absorbing Probability</a> of state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Markov_Chain_Frog.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/0/03/Markov_Chain_Frog.png/950px-Markov_Chain_Frog.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"950\" height=\"108\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/0/03/Markov_Chain_Frog.png/1425px-Markov_Chain_Frog.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/0/03/Markov_Chain_Frog.png/1900px-Markov_Chain_Frog.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/c/76ce4b055db58eaf041c6df81590b7303d886cf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{ij} = P(X_{n+1} = j | X_n = i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"209\" height=\"20\" />, the probability that state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> transits to state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> on the next step. \n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> be the absorbing probability of being absorbed in the absorbing state when starting from transient state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/8/df8f3396927514bb3df77874fb9c6249d4157480.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i = \\sum_{j} (p_{ij} \\cdot a_{j})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"132\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is the sum of the products of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/79220e9b9f82ca92de037d2a8a6bc00ac0f9a0e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{ij}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/0785f19824c8b23ab3dfe000b471994078fdf589.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> of all the next state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Isabelchen\" title=\"User:Isabelchen\">isabelchen</a>\n</p><p>Note:\n</p><p>This sum results in the same system of equations as solution 2, and the solution follows as well. \n</p><p>~POCPOC44\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DMdgh2mMiWM\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DMdgh2mMiWM</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_22&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/QqeaomXYDsg\">https://youtu.be/QqeaomXYDsg</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a 